      </itemfeedback>
    </item>
    <item ident="ga5bf36ae4ec3b947668be103410ae496" title="Question 13">
      <itemmetadata>
        <qtimetadata>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>question_type</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>multiple_choice_question</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>points_possible</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>1.0</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>original_answer_ids</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>20857,1566,15514,50526</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
        </qtimetadata>
      </itemmetadata>
      <presentation>
        <material>
          <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;What is the bandwagon logical fallacy?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</mattext>
        </material>
        <response_lid ident="response1" rcardinality="Single">
          <render_choice>
            <response_label ident="20857">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Arguing that something is correct because many people believe or do it</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
            <response_label ident="1566">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Attacking the personal character of someone making an argument</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
            <response_label ident="15514">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Assuming that one event automatically caused another event</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
            <response_label ident="50526">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Presenting an exaggerated claim that distorts the truth</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
          </render_choice>
        </response_lid>
      </presentation>
      <resprocessing>
        <outcomes>
          <decvar maxvalue="100" minvalue="0" varname="SCORE" vartype="Decimal"/>
        </outcomes>
        <respcondition continue="Yes">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">1566</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <displayfeedback feedbacktype="Response" linkrefid="1566_fb"/>
        </respcondition>
        <respcondition continue="Yes">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">15514</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <displayfeedback feedbacktype="Response" linkrefid="15514_fb"/>
        </respcondition>
        <respcondition continue="Yes">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">50526</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <displayfeedback feedbacktype="Response" linkrefid="50526_fb"/>
        </respcondition>
        <respcondition continue="No">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">20857</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <setvar action="Set" varname="SCORE">100</setvar>
        </respcondition>
      </resprocessing>
      <itemfeedback ident="1566_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Attacking character describes ad hominem, assuming cause-and-effect without proof describes a causal fallacy, and exaggerating claims describes hyperbole. The bandwagon fallacy argues that because many people believe or do something, you should too—using popularity as a substitute for real evidence.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="15514_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Attacking character describes ad hominem, assuming cause-and-effect without proof describes a causal fallacy, and exaggerating claims describes hyperbole. The bandwagon fallacy argues that because many people believe or do something, you should too—using popularity as a substitute for real evidence.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="50526_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Attacking character describes ad hominem, assuming cause-and-effect without proof describes a causal fallacy, and exaggerating claims describes hyperbole. The bandwagon fallacy argues that because many people believe or do something, you should too—using popularity as a substitute for real evidence.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
    </item>
    <item ident="ga50989b4d55587e90beac042b765ea28" title="Question 14">
      <itemmetadata>
        <qtimetadata>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>question_type</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>multiple_choice_question</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>points_possible</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>1</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>original_answer_ids</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>12546,18813,40323,32175</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
        </qtimetadata>
      </itemmetadata>
      <presentation>
        <material>
          <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;div&gt;What is the slippery slope logical fallacy?&lt;/div&gt;</mattext>
        </material>
        <response_lid ident="response1" rcardinality="Single">
          <render_choice>
            <response_label ident="12546">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Assuming one action will automatically lead to a chain of extreme consequences</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
            <response_label ident="18813">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Offering only two choices when other options exist</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
            <response_label ident="40323">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Using popularity to argue that something must be correct</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
            <response_label ident="32175">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Attacking the identity of the person making an argument</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
          </render_choice>
        </response_lid>
      </presentation>
      <resprocessing>
        <outcomes>
          <decvar maxvalue="100" minvalue="0" varname="SCORE" vartype="Decimal"/>
        </outcomes>
        <respcondition continue="Yes">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">18813</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <displayfeedback feedbacktype="Response" linkrefid="18813_fb"/>
        </respcondition>
        <respcondition continue="Yes">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">40323</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <displayfeedback feedbacktype="Response" linkrefid="40323_fb"/>
        </respcondition>
        <respcondition continue="Yes">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">32175</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <displayfeedback feedbacktype="Response" linkrefid="32175_fb"/>
        </respcondition>
        <respcondition continue="No">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">12546</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <setvar action="Set" varname="SCORE">100</setvar>
        </respcondition>
      </resprocessing>
      <itemfeedback ident="18813_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/plain">Offering only two choices describes a false dilemma, using popularity describes the bandwagon fallacy, and attacking a person describes ad hominem. The slippery slope fallacy assumes that one action will automatically trigger a series of extreme outcomes, without showing how or why each step would occur.</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="40323_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/plain">Offering only two choices describes a false dilemma, using popularity describes the bandwagon fallacy, and attacking a person describes ad hominem. The slippery slope fallacy assumes that one action will automatically trigger a series of extreme outcomes, without showing how or why each step would occur.</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="32175_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/plain">Offering only two choices describes a false dilemma, using popularity describes the bandwagon fallacy, and attacking a person describes ad hominem. The slippery slope fallacy assumes that one action will automatically trigger a series of extreme outcomes, without showing how or why each step would occur.</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
    </item>
    <item ident="gf5f5ee70e966a7bbdf2ed9d08210d11a" title="Question 15">
      <itemmetadata>
        <qtimetadata>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>question_type</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>multiple_choice_question</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
          <qtimetadatafield>
