    <item ident="g8ddcadd89e46ff31be8d1776ba4b5745" title="Question 47">
      <itemmetadata>
        <qtimetadata>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>question_type</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>multiple_choice_question</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>points_possible</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>1.0</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
          <qtimetadatafield>
            <fieldlabel>original_answer_ids</fieldlabel>
            <fieldentry>78106,97296,4967,99673</fieldentry>
          </qtimetadatafield>
        </qtimetadata>
      </itemmetadata>
      <presentation>
        <material>
          <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;What is a key characteristic of effective persuasive messages that combine multiple rhetorical appeals?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</mattext>
        </material>
        <response_lid ident="response1" rcardinality="Single">
          <render_choice>
            <response_label ident="78106">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Each appeal adds a different dimension to the message, making it stronger overall</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
            <response_label ident="97296">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Using multiple appeals makes the message longer and more difficult to read</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
            <response_label ident="4967">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Writers should use all four appeals equally in every persuasive message</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
            <response_label ident="99673">
              <material>
                <mattext texttype="text/plain">Combining appeals is only appropriate in formal written documents</mattext>
              </material>
            </response_label>
          </render_choice>
        </response_lid>
      </presentation>
      <resprocessing>
        <outcomes>
          <decvar maxvalue="100" minvalue="0" varname="SCORE" vartype="Decimal"/>
        </outcomes>
        <respcondition continue="Yes">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">97296</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <displayfeedback feedbacktype="Response" linkrefid="97296_fb"/>
        </respcondition>
        <respcondition continue="Yes">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">4967</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <displayfeedback feedbacktype="Response" linkrefid="4967_fb"/>
        </respcondition>
        <respcondition continue="Yes">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">99673</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <displayfeedback feedbacktype="Response" linkrefid="99673_fb"/>
        </respcondition>
        <respcondition continue="No">
          <conditionvar>
            <varequal respident="response1">78106</varequal>
          </conditionvar>
          <setvar action="Set" varname="SCORE">100</setvar>
        </respcondition>
      </resprocessing>
      <itemfeedback ident="97296_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Length and difficulty are not the result of combining appeals, equal use of all four appeals in every message is not required, and combining appeals works effectively in brief messages as well as formal documents. Combining multiple rhetorical appeals strengthens a persuasive message because each appeal—ethos, pathos, logos, and kairos—contributes a different dimension, creating a more complete and compelling argument for the audience.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
      <itemfeedback ident="4967_fb">
        <flow_mat>
          <material>
            <mattext texttype="text/html">&lt;p&gt;Length and difficulty are not the result of combining appeals, equal use of all four appeals in every message is not required, and combining appeals works effectively in brief messages as well as formal documents. Combining multiple rhetorical appeals strengthens a persuasive message because each appeal—ethos, pathos, logos, and kairos—contributes a different dimension, creating a more complete and compelling argument for the audience.&lt;/p&gt;</mattext>
          </material>
        </flow_mat>
      </itemfeedback>
